1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,985
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,985 --> 00:00:02,975
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:02,975 --> 00:00:05,945
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:15,600
SARA BEERY: So why are we all here?

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:21,200
Deep learning has clearly been exploding in society.

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,400
Machine learning generally is something

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:28,040
that, when I started studying it about 13 years ago, didn't work,

8
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:30,360
and now it works.

9
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:35,360
So how many of you here in this room used AI in the last week?

10
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,560
Yeah, almost everybody, probably everybody.

11
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:43,920
And we're seeing massive advances in everything from

12
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:49,940
AI-assisted text generation to 3D reconstruction,

13
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:55,460
things like NeRF, things like AlphaGo, generating images,

14
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:00,890
helping with writing code, playing games.

15
00:01:00,890 --> 00:01:03,850
This has really started to touch almost every dimension

16
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:08,370
of society, and it's definitely very exciting to see.

17
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:10,090
And I think that hopefully, all of us

18
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:14,570
will learn a lot more about what that means in this course.

19
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:17,010
So what is deep learning?

20
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:22,250
First, I would argue that one necessary component

21
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:25,290
of deep learning is the idea of neural networks.

22
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:28,290
And these are a class of machine learning architectures

23
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:31,490
that basically use stacks of linear transformations

24
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:35,570
interleaved with pointwise non-linearities.

25
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:39,330
And these have really been a building block

26
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:43,090
for a lot of the progress that we saw in the last slide.

27
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:45,650
But the other really important dimension of deep learning

28
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:48,250
is the idea of differential programming.

29
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:50,810
And this is essentially a programming paradigm

30
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:53,810
where we parameterize parts of the program

31
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:56,490
and then let gradient-based optimization

32
00:01:56,490 --> 00:01:59,510
tune the parameters to basically optimize that program

33
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:04,190
or find some of, at least, local optimal for that program.

34
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:06,510
And these two things together is what

35
00:02:06,510 --> 00:02:08,392
we posit make up deep learning.

36
00:02:08,392 --> 00:02:10,350
And we're really going to dig into both of them

37
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:11,990
in this course.

38
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:14,990
So our philosophy for this is that breakthroughs

39
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:18,350
in deep learning have been driven by a mixture of theory

40
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:20,710
and practice, and both dimensions

41
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:24,670
are really vital for future progress in the field.

42
00:02:24,670 --> 00:02:27,190
And so along those lines, this course

43
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:30,030
should hopefully provide both a theoretical grounding

44
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,110
in important deep learning building blocks,

45
00:02:33,110 --> 00:02:37,390
as well as give you practice implementing, understanding,

46
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:40,190
and using those blocks.

47
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:42,270
OK, there's still a lot of people

48
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:44,610
in the back who are packed in.

49
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:47,510
I don't know if there's any way to come further forward.

50
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:51,150
If not, we'll do our best.

51
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:56,110
So the class's coursework is going to be 65% problem sets.

52
00:02:56,110 --> 00:02:57,290
You'll have five psets.

53
00:02:57,290 --> 00:02:59,920
Each will be about one to two weeks long.

54
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,300
And then there will be some combination of pen and paper

55
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,240
or, more realistically, maybe Overleaf,

56
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:07,360
as well as code that you will need to do and submit.

57
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,058
And then 35% of the grade will be a final project.

58
00:03:10,058 --> 00:03:11,600
And this is a research project that's

59
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:13,880
focused on deeper understanding of some of the topics

60
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,920
that we cover in the course.

61
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,940
You'll be asked to propose something for that project.

62
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:21,640
So there'll be an initial project proposal.

63
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:24,200
And then the final project will be a blog post

64
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:26,360
that's going to demonstrate novel experimentation

65
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,680
and visualization.

66
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:29,920
And the hope here is that I don't know,

67
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,760
many of you-- how many of you are machine learning researchers

68
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,160
who have already written a machine learning paper,

69
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:37,580
as first author, or anywhere on the author list?

70
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:39,413
OK, so some.

71
00:03:39,413 --> 00:03:41,580
These days, when you write a machine learning paper,

72
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:43,040
more often than not, you also have

73
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:45,320
to write a blog post about that paper.

74
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:47,640
And that's intended to be catchy, make

75
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:52,020
people understand the material, but really get them engaged.

76
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,120
And so one of the reasons that we have this

77
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,880
as the format of our final project

78
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:59,250
is to reflect on the fact that this is the reality we live

79
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:01,310
in today, that in machine learning research,

80
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:05,450
being able to write a blog-style description of the work

81
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,610
that you're doing and not reduce any

82
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,970
of the technical complexity or the information

83
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:13,130
that's given across, but just do this in this slightly

84
00:04:13,130 --> 00:04:15,810
more visual and maybe even interactive format is

85
00:04:15,810 --> 00:04:20,250
a really valuable skill, and one that we hope this can help us

86
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:21,170
think about.

87
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:25,850
And we'll allow groups, but only up to two.

88
00:04:25,850 --> 00:04:28,250
And another important component--

89
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:29,990
we are not able to provide compute.

90
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:32,990
We may be able to provide some very limited compute,

91
00:04:32,990 --> 00:04:34,570
but that's TBD.

92
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:37,010
So do not plan your final project

93
00:04:37,010 --> 00:04:39,370
to be something where you need to get state of the art

94
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:44,250
by crunching a huge architecture on a bunch of huge data sets,

95
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:46,010
because it's just not going to work.

96
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:48,410
You're not going to be able to outcompete OpenAI

97
00:04:48,410 --> 00:04:50,030
on this research project.

98
00:04:50,030 --> 00:04:50,530
[LAUGHS]

99
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:54,770
So this does not mean you can't do good research.

100
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:56,970
And it's important to get creative here.

101
00:04:56,970 --> 00:04:59,190
And I think it's actually a really valuable skill,

102
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:03,030
particularly as certain types of large-scale deep learning become

103
00:05:03,030 --> 00:05:06,470
more and more centralized, to think about how you can still do

104
00:05:06,470 --> 00:05:09,830
impactful machine learning research in a way that is not

105
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:13,230
just "bigger is better."

106
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:18,070
All right, any questions about coursework or logistics?

107
00:05:18,070 --> 00:05:18,870
Yeah.

108
00:05:18,870 --> 00:05:20,790
AUDIENCE: Is the final project expected

109
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:24,807
to be a mix of practical and theoretical research?

110
00:05:24,807 --> 00:05:27,390
SARA BEERY: So the question was, is the final project expected

111
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:30,710
to be a mix of practical and theoretical research?

112
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:32,290
I think it depends.

113
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:35,590
It depends on what you want to propose as your final project.

114
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:37,390
But I think we're pretty open.

115
00:05:37,390 --> 00:05:39,610
What we want is for them to be innovative,

116
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:42,410
but that innovation can be in an applied sense,

117
00:05:42,410 --> 00:05:44,250
or it can be in a more theoretical sense.

118
00:05:44,250 --> 00:05:46,550
OK, so I'm going to do a really high-level overview

119
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:50,670
of the schedule, just to give you a rough sense.

120
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:53,960
So today, we're really covering basic interest stuff-- course

121
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:57,760
overview, a rough introduction to what neural networks are

122
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,600
and basic building blocks, and, particularly

123
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:01,058
in the context of signposting, what

124
00:06:01,058 --> 00:06:03,560
we expect you've seen before, because this is not an intro

125
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:04,660
to deep learning class.

126
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:08,680
This is advanced graduate-level deep learning.

127
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:12,040
And then we're going to go into how you actually

128
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:14,800
train a neural net, then approximation

129
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,360
theory, some different architectures, things

130
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,960
like grids, graphs.

131
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:22,640
Then we'll hear from Jeremy about scaling rules

132
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:23,980
for optimization.

133
00:06:23,980 --> 00:06:26,720
And then we'll look at generalization theory, more

134
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:29,040
architectures, getting into transformers.

135
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:32,400
We'll do a really fun lecture by Phil called the hacker's guide

136
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:35,400
to deep learning that I think is quite useful.

137
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:37,440
And then we'll talk about memory.

138
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,697
And then we'll get into representation learning.

139
00:06:39,697 --> 00:06:42,280
So there, we're going to look at reconstruction based and then

140
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:45,560
similarity based and then theory of representation learning.

141
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:48,460
Then we'll have a little bit of a series on generative models,

142
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:51,520
the basics, how representation learning interacts

143
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:53,610
with generative modeling, thinking

144
00:06:53,610 --> 00:06:55,440
about conditional models.

145
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:57,690
Then we'll talk about generalization, and particularly

146
00:06:57,690 --> 00:06:59,810
out-of-distribution generalization.

147
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:01,490
We'll touch on transfer learning,

148
00:07:01,490 --> 00:07:04,050
both from a models and a data perspective.

149
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:06,525
We'll hear about large language models.

150
00:07:06,525 --> 00:07:09,150
We'll have some guest lectures towards the end of the semester,

151
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:10,970
and those are TBD.

152
00:07:10,970 --> 00:07:14,010
Then we'll hear about scaling laws, automatic gradient

153
00:07:14,010 --> 00:07:16,063
descent, and potentially the past and future

154
00:07:16,063 --> 00:07:17,730
of deep learning, though I think there's

155
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:19,730
some discussion as to whether that's going

156
00:07:19,730 --> 00:07:22,130
to be a different lecture.

157
00:07:22,130 --> 00:07:24,410
And then, I think kind of importantly,

158
00:07:24,410 --> 00:07:28,102
right towards the end, we will have class-wide project office

159
00:07:28,102 --> 00:07:29,810
hours where we'll have a bunch of the TAs

160
00:07:29,810 --> 00:07:31,490
and the instructors all come, and we

161
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:36,130
can do open discussion around projects before they're due.

162
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:40,330
So the next thing is that we're going to host PyTorch tutorials.

163
00:07:40,330 --> 00:07:43,050
This is intended for if you're not familiar with PyTorch,

164
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:44,830
and you think you need a refresher.

165
00:07:44,830 --> 00:07:47,953
And we strongly recommend that if this is the case,

166
00:07:47,953 --> 00:07:50,370
or if you even think it might be the case, that you attend

167
00:07:50,370 --> 00:07:53,070
one of the two PyTorch tutorials we're going to offer next week.

168
00:07:53,070 --> 00:07:54,990
So the question is if PyTorch is a requirement,

169
00:07:54,990 --> 00:07:56,490
or you're allowed to use any other frameworks.

170
00:07:56,490 --> 00:07:58,830
So you're probably welcome to use any other frameworks

171
00:07:58,830 --> 00:08:01,150
in your final project, but some of the problem sets

172
00:08:01,150 --> 00:08:03,630
specifically will have code that's

173
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:07,030
already built into PyTorch, and you're filling parts of that in.

174
00:08:07,030 --> 00:08:08,870
So unless you want to rewrite all of it

175
00:08:08,870 --> 00:08:11,850
in JAX or something, which more power to you,

176
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:13,830
I would recommend that you definitely

177
00:08:13,830 --> 00:08:17,910
make sure that you are familiar with PyTorch.

178
00:08:17,910 --> 00:08:19,648
OK.

179
00:08:19,648 --> 00:08:21,690
Cool, so I'm going to talk about course policies.

180
00:08:21,690 --> 00:08:23,350
And this stuff is important.

181
00:08:23,350 --> 00:08:25,710
And it's also all on the web page.

182
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:28,590
But I think essentially, what we want to touch on

183
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:31,230
is what our collaboration policy is first.

184
00:08:31,230 --> 00:08:34,950
So everyone is required to write an individual pset.

185
00:08:34,950 --> 00:08:37,169
So when you're actually working on your problem sets,

186
00:08:37,169 --> 00:08:39,030
do not work on something together and then

187
00:08:39,030 --> 00:08:41,010
both submit the exact same thing.

188
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:46,170
You can work on the actual-- the content,

189
00:08:46,170 --> 00:08:48,450
you can discuss that with peers, TAs, and instructors,

190
00:08:48,450 --> 00:08:49,950
but the problem sets you submit need

191
00:08:49,950 --> 00:08:53,080
to be your own independent and individual work.

192
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:55,740
And that also applies to the code that you write.

193
00:08:55,740 --> 00:08:57,420
So you can discuss the code with others,

194
00:08:57,420 --> 00:08:59,860
but you need to each write your own code separately.

195
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:01,860
There was some confusion about this in the past.

196
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:04,760
Just make sure that you're not taking the code someone else

197
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:08,680
wrote, running it, and then submitting a pset on it.

198
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:11,800
You should absolutely not copy or share complete solutions,

199
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,640
and you also should not ask someone else, hey, this

200
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:16,640
is my solution, is it right?

201
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:18,140
And you shouldn't do that in person,

202
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:20,200
and you also shouldn't ask, Is this

203
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:22,520
correct? on Piazza or Canvas.

204
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:24,660
So again, the idea is, it's your independent work,

205
00:09:24,660 --> 00:09:26,560
your independent thought.

206
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,440
And I think it matters more that you've gone through that process

207
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:31,720
independently than actually getting it correct

208
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:34,698
because you'll learn from the feedback on your psets

209
00:09:34,698 --> 00:09:37,240
better if you submit something that you came up with yourself

210
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:41,320
and get feedback on it than if you copy somebody.

211
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:44,117
And then if you work with anyone on the pset other than TAs

212
00:09:44,117 --> 00:09:46,200
and instructors, we ask that you write their names

213
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,100
at the top of the pset.

214
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:49,020
So if you have a study group, you're all working together--

215
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:50,600
we don't care if this is 10 people.

216
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:52,960
We don't care if it's, in this class, 100 people.

217
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,180
But we just want to know who was working together.

218
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:58,580
I'd be impressed if you managed to come up with a 100-person

219
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:00,980
collaborative group.

220
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:06,180
So AI assistance-- we take the exact same policy

221
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:08,900
about things like ChatGPT or other AI assistants

222
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,740
that we do with humans.

223
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:13,000
So it's a deep learning class.

224
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,380
You should be trying out the latest technology.

225
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:17,900
The idea is not that we're teaching

226
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,540
you to ignore what's happening.

227
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:25,320
But we do want you to treat them like a human collaborator.

228
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:27,720
Don't ask them to answer the question for you.

229
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:31,020
Just try to imagine from an ethics perspective

230
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:33,460
that this is like a peer in the class.

231
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:35,400
So don't say, what is the solution to this?

232
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,940
Don't say, write the code for me,

233
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:38,523
because you wouldn't go to your friend

234
00:10:38,523 --> 00:10:41,140
and say, please write my code for me.

235
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:45,840
But you're super welcome to use them as a discussion partner.

236
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:49,000
Ask them questions that are contextual.

237
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,290
Go back and forth.

238
00:10:50,290 --> 00:10:51,070
That's all fine.

239
00:10:51,070 --> 00:10:56,150
But don't treat AI collaboration as if they're not

240
00:10:56,150 --> 00:10:57,350
a human collaborator.

241
00:10:57,350 --> 00:10:58,670
Think of it that way first.

242
00:10:58,670 --> 00:11:02,392
Is this something I would ask my friend in the class?

243
00:11:02,392 --> 00:11:04,350
And then just like you can come to office hours

244
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:06,850
and ask a human questions about the lecture

245
00:11:06,850 --> 00:11:09,030
material, clarifications about questions, tips

246
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:11,310
for getting started, you're welcome to do the same

247
00:11:11,310 --> 00:11:13,590
with AI assistants.

248
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:16,150
Again, you're not allowed to ask an expert friend

249
00:11:16,150 --> 00:11:17,430
to do your homework for you.

250
00:11:17,430 --> 00:11:21,110
Don't ask AI to do your homework for you.

251
00:11:21,110 --> 00:11:24,470
If you're ever unclear, imagine the AI is a human

252
00:11:24,470 --> 00:11:26,510
and apply that same norm.

253
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:30,455
And if you work with any AI on a pset, similarly to the fact

254
00:11:30,455 --> 00:11:31,830
that we're writing with humans we

255
00:11:31,830 --> 00:11:34,670
collaborated with, please write down which AI

256
00:11:34,670 --> 00:11:36,310
and how you used it.

257
00:11:36,310 --> 00:11:38,890
And, of course, this is a bit vague, but again,

258
00:11:38,890 --> 00:11:41,790
just kind of honor code.

259
00:11:41,790 --> 00:11:45,150
Any questions about AI collaboration,

260
00:11:45,150 --> 00:11:48,520
or is that reasonably clear?

261
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:49,220
Yeah.

262
00:11:49,220 --> 00:11:51,380
AUDIENCE: I had a question on the last page.

263
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:55,053
So can we show our work to the TAs to [INAUDIBLE]?

264
00:11:55,053 --> 00:11:55,720
SARA BEERY: Yes.

265
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:58,785
You're very welcome to show your work to the TAs.

266
00:11:58,785 --> 00:12:00,160
The TAs are not going to tell you

267
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:01,618
how to answer the question, though,

268
00:12:01,618 --> 00:12:05,120
but they will work with you to help you hopefully figure it out

269
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:06,480
on your own.

270
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:07,920
Why are we here?

271
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:10,880
[LAUGHS] What's the goal?

272
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:12,640
So what do we want to do?

273
00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:17,640
Why are we all interested in things like deep learning?

274
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:20,680
One perspective is that we're interested in modeling

275
00:12:20,680 --> 00:12:24,320
complex phenomena in the real world.

276
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:26,400
What are complex phenomena?

277
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:28,220
Well, you interact with them every day,

278
00:12:28,220 --> 00:12:30,800
things like natural language, images,

279
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:33,560
or visual data in general, videos.

280
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:37,140
DNA, ecosystems are complex phenomena.

281
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:40,280
Climate change is a complex phenomenon.

282
00:12:40,280 --> 00:12:44,720
Why is it hard to model complex phenomena in the real world?

283
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:46,320
They're complex.

284
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:51,300
[LAUGHS] And so maybe one existence proof

285
00:12:51,300 --> 00:12:54,740
for deep learning as a solution to modeling complex phenomena--

286
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:57,160
possibly, we could talk about the human brain.

287
00:12:57,160 --> 00:12:59,210
All of you are here at MIT, which

288
00:12:59,210 --> 00:13:01,460
means you're pretty good at modeling complex phenomena

289
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:02,880
in your brain, I'm guessing.

290
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:10,780
So first, today, I'm going to just briefly go over

291
00:13:10,780 --> 00:13:12,240
how we got where we are today.

292
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:15,140
This is a brief history of the field of AI,

293
00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:17,060
deep learning in general.

294
00:13:17,060 --> 00:13:19,140
And then I'm going to go over what we expect

295
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:21,100
you have already seen before.

296
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:23,240
It's OK if you haven't seen these things before,

297
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:26,500
but we would expect you then to go and brush up on them.

298
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:28,940
So if there's anything that I talk about, and you're like,

299
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:31,640
oh, I have never seen that, we expect that.

300
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:34,700
That's something you can go look at some of the OpenCourseWare

301
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:39,180
courses at MIT, go find lots of resources online, and pick it up

302
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:40,903
before you go into this class.

303
00:13:40,903 --> 00:13:42,820
And then I'll also talk about what we actually

304
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:45,190
cover in the class.

305
00:13:45,190 --> 00:13:47,290
So a brief history of neural networks--

306
00:13:47,290 --> 00:13:49,830
and I think a fun way to go through this

307
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:52,790
is looking at this on a plot of enthusiasm

308
00:13:52,790 --> 00:13:55,030
for neural networks over time.

309
00:13:55,030 --> 00:13:58,990
So if we go way back to 1958, Rosenblatt

310
00:13:58,990 --> 00:14:04,270
introduced the perceptron in Psychological Review.

311
00:14:04,270 --> 00:14:09,750
And the perceptron was the first neural network, arguably.

312
00:14:09,750 --> 00:14:11,310
And what this was intending to do

313
00:14:11,310 --> 00:14:15,790
was recognize or categorize images.

314
00:14:15,790 --> 00:14:18,550
And the idea was essentially that you

315
00:14:18,550 --> 00:14:22,590
could take some combination of representations--

316
00:14:22,590 --> 00:14:25,390
in this case, particularly pixels--

317
00:14:25,390 --> 00:14:28,310
sum them, put them through some non-linearity,

318
00:14:28,310 --> 00:14:30,910
and get out a categorization.

319
00:14:30,910 --> 00:14:32,990
And really, we'll see throughout the course

320
00:14:32,990 --> 00:14:37,430
and even today, this idea still forms the building block

321
00:14:37,430 --> 00:14:39,610
for most of the deep learning that we do.

322
00:14:39,610 --> 00:14:42,830
And these perceptrons are components

323
00:14:42,830 --> 00:14:44,800
in many, many, many different types

324
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:48,000
of capacities within things like ChatGPT.

325
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,740
When this paper came out back in 1958,

326
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:52,680
people were incredibly enthusiastic.

327
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:55,800
It made waves.

328
00:14:55,800 --> 00:15:00,000
And then in 1972, Minsky and Papert

329
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,880
wrote a book called Perceptrons, Expanded Edition.

330
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:07,280
And this book takes a very critical lens to the idea

331
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,480
that a perceptron could be seriously considered

332
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:10,880
a model of the brain.

333
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:14,560
And it also carefully and mathematically describes

334
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:16,720
and characterizes the limitations

335
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:19,280
of what a perceptron can model.

336
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:22,600
And it did so, so carefully and so critically

337
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:26,400
that all of a sudden, enthusiasm for machine learning really

338
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:27,680
took a dip.

339
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:32,720
So in the '70s, we were not excited about neural networks.

340
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:37,880
But then in 1986, this book came out called Parallel Distributed

341
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:38,840
Processing.

342
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:42,160
And this was fundamental because it introduces

343
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:45,100
the concept of backpropagation.

344
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:47,020
And this was really the thing that

345
00:15:47,020 --> 00:15:49,780
enabled multilayer perceptrons, so not just

346
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:51,460
a single perceptron, but actually

347
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,540
multiple perceptrons stacked on top of each other

348
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:57,860
to actually be reasonably trained, reasonably fit,

349
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:00,180
this concept of being able to backpropagate

350
00:16:00,180 --> 00:16:03,580
a gradient through multiple layers of perceptrons.

351
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:05,460
And what this enabled you to solve

352
00:16:05,460 --> 00:16:07,793
were things like the XOR problem.

353
00:16:07,793 --> 00:16:09,460
For the first time, you could reasonably

354
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:11,140
solve categorization problems that

355
00:16:11,140 --> 00:16:14,660
were not separable from a linear classifier.

356
00:16:14,660 --> 00:16:16,980
They pointed to backpropagation as a breakthrough.

357
00:16:16,980 --> 00:16:20,580
It led us actually handle things where

358
00:16:20,580 --> 00:16:25,820
you need multiple different boundaries and these nonlinear

359
00:16:25,820 --> 00:16:28,580
boundaries to be drawn, which is only possible if you

360
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,540
have multiple layers of a perceptron.

361
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:32,820
You'll never be able to solve the XOR

362
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:36,620
problem with a single-layer neural network.

363
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:38,860
And everyone got really excited again.

364
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:42,575
So in the '80s, yet again, machine learning was something

365
00:16:42,575 --> 00:16:44,950
that people were pumped about, and particularly this deep

366
00:16:44,950 --> 00:16:48,190
learning neural network perspective on machine learning.

367
00:16:48,190 --> 00:16:54,150
In 1998, Yann LeCun put out convolutional neural networks.

368
00:16:54,150 --> 00:16:56,830
There's an awesome demo that he has on his web page.

369
00:16:56,830 --> 00:17:00,270
I'm sure many of you have heard of Yann LeCun.

370
00:17:00,270 --> 00:17:04,390
And this seems like it would mean

371
00:17:04,390 --> 00:17:07,750
that everyone was really excited about neural networks again.

372
00:17:07,750 --> 00:17:11,069
But if you go to NeurIPS in 2000,

373
00:17:11,069 --> 00:17:14,310
this is the premier conference on machine learning.

374
00:17:14,310 --> 00:17:16,390
It evolved from an interdisciplinary conference,

375
00:17:16,390 --> 00:17:22,470
which was on basically the science of the brain

376
00:17:22,470 --> 00:17:25,710
and machine learning to specifically just a machine

377
00:17:25,710 --> 00:17:27,109
learning conference.

378
00:17:27,109 --> 00:17:31,510
If you look at that conference in 2000,

379
00:17:31,510 --> 00:17:35,030
the most predictive words in papers in the title

380
00:17:35,030 --> 00:17:39,550
for acceptance were "belief propagation" and "Gaussian."

381
00:17:39,550 --> 00:17:43,080
And the title words most predictive of paper rejection

382
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:45,720
were "neural" and "network."

383
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:47,080
[LAUGHTER]

384
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:49,940
So this is what we called the AI winter.

385
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:51,440
And one of the reasons it was called

386
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:54,060
the AI winter is because even though we had the theory,

387
00:17:54,060 --> 00:17:56,520
we had the building blocks, we didn't actually

388
00:17:56,520 --> 00:18:01,960
have the ability to train them efficiently.

389
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:04,560
We didn't have the right programming perspective,

390
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:07,680
and we didn't have the right hardware.

391
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:12,800
But then in 2012, Alex Krizhevsky and his co-authors

392
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:14,360
published AlexNet.

393
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:20,040
And AlexNet was also another convolutional neural network.

394
00:18:20,040 --> 00:18:24,600
But importantly, Alex Krizhevsky was a brilliant programmer,

395
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:26,320
and Alex Krizhevsky figured out how

396
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:29,240
to program GPUs, graphics processing units, that

397
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:33,120
have been developed to handle massive-scale parallel

398
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:37,960
multiplications for graphics, for video games,

399
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:44,100
and for high-resolution images.

400
00:18:44,100 --> 00:18:46,460
But he figured out how to program those and repurposed

401
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:50,220
that hardware for the training of neural networks.

402
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:53,660
And I can't tell you how much of a shot heard around the world

403
00:18:53,660 --> 00:18:55,540
this was in our field.

404
00:18:55,540 --> 00:18:59,940
But this, for the first time, outperformed

405
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:03,420
every single other possible method

406
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:06,780
that was out there on ImageNet.

407
00:19:06,780 --> 00:19:09,380
ImageNet-- how many of you have heard of ImageNet?

408
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:10,340
Yeah.

409
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:12,800
ImageNet was the first very large-scale data

410
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,060
set of its kind.

411
00:19:14,060 --> 00:19:16,180
And I actually think that this is

412
00:19:16,180 --> 00:19:18,260
an important component, what made

413
00:19:18,260 --> 00:19:20,140
machine learning start to work.

414
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:22,160
And I would argue that, of course,

415
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:25,760
it was the theory and the programming,

416
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:29,300
the ability to actually efficiently fit these things.

417
00:19:29,300 --> 00:19:33,900
But the other really vital component was the data.

418
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:37,360
Machine learning does not work without large-scale, curated,

419
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:40,607
labeled data in many capacities still,

420
00:19:40,607 --> 00:19:42,190
though we're getting better and better

421
00:19:42,190 --> 00:19:44,150
at self-supervised learning.

422
00:19:44,150 --> 00:19:48,910
But so all of a sudden, we were back.

423
00:19:48,910 --> 00:19:50,450
Everyone was enthusiastic again.

424
00:19:50,450 --> 00:19:52,470
And actually, if you look, these were

425
00:19:52,470 --> 00:19:56,110
hype cycles of about 28 years, both of them.

426
00:19:56,110 --> 00:20:00,990
And so the question is, where are we going to be in 2028?

427
00:20:00,990 --> 00:20:04,350
So it's 2024 now.

428
00:20:04,350 --> 00:20:08,030
It's 2024, so in four years, where are we going to be?

429
00:20:08,030 --> 00:20:11,610
So one argument would be, OK, we're in another hype cycle.

430
00:20:11,610 --> 00:20:15,030
But I would actually argue that that's probably not true.

431
00:20:15,030 --> 00:20:17,990
Another one would be that we're just way

432
00:20:17,990 --> 00:20:19,850
off the deep end in terms of enthusiasm,

433
00:20:19,850 --> 00:20:23,830
and by 2028, we'll be in some-- out in the stratosphere.

434
00:20:23,830 --> 00:20:26,710
Or possibly we're doing something like this.

435
00:20:26,710 --> 00:20:28,430
Maybe in 2028, we're going to hit

436
00:20:28,430 --> 00:20:31,452
some new plane of enthusiasm, and then

437
00:20:31,452 --> 00:20:32,910
we'll start to realize limitations,

438
00:20:32,910 --> 00:20:34,350
and we'll oscillate again.

439
00:20:34,350 --> 00:20:35,696
Yeah?

440
00:20:35,696 --> 00:20:38,340
AUDIENCE: Could AI take over society by 2028?

441
00:20:38,340 --> 00:20:39,840
SARA BEERY: [LAUGHS] So the question

442
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:42,640
is, will AI take over society by 2028?

443
00:20:42,640 --> 00:20:44,560
My answer is, let's see.

444
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:46,160
[LAUGHS]

445
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:47,800
So what's deep learning today?

446
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:51,560
What are the components that make it up?

447
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:55,080
First, I think a really important one is Autograd.

448
00:20:55,080 --> 00:20:58,445
These are coding languages like PyTorch and TensorFlow

449
00:20:58,445 --> 00:21:00,320
that give us a really clever way to do things

450
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:04,040
like implement the chain rule in software, making

451
00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:06,480
good use of available hardware.

452
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:09,420
And this has enabled us to do things

453
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:11,940
like approximate the gradient of any function,

454
00:21:11,940 --> 00:21:14,080
even if it's an approximation.

455
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:17,140
These are a really important component of deep learning.

456
00:21:17,140 --> 00:21:19,383
And if you do anything with deep learning,

457
00:21:19,383 --> 00:21:21,300
I doubt that you're doing what Krizhevsky did.

458
00:21:21,300 --> 00:21:23,260
I doubt that you're programming these things from scratch.

459
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:25,635
You're almost certainly working with existing programming

460
00:21:25,635 --> 00:21:28,640
languages that are built for machine learning.

461
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:32,678
Its billion-plus data point datasets, things like LAION.

462
00:21:32,678 --> 00:21:34,720
So that scale of data that I talked about before,

463
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:37,740
this is an incredibly important component of all of this.

464
00:21:37,740 --> 00:21:41,820
Parallel training on thousands of GPUs--

465
00:21:41,820 --> 00:21:45,060
again, this is what we're looking at today--

466
00:21:45,060 --> 00:21:46,920
billion-plus parameter architectures,

467
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:51,020
and this number just keeps increasing, million-plus

468
00:21:51,020 --> 00:21:52,900
dollar training costs.

469
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:55,300
So, of course, it's not public knowledge

470
00:21:55,300 --> 00:21:57,980
how much it costs to train something like GPT 4.0.

471
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:03,900
But I would bet it's definitely in the many millions.

472
00:22:03,900 --> 00:22:06,467
Shockingly good results-- I don't know about any of you.

473
00:22:06,467 --> 00:22:08,800
I mean, I've been working in this field now for a while,

474
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:13,380
and I think we've been seeing results recently that I didn't

475
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:16,860
anticipate seeing in my lifetime from when I started, back when

476
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:18,380
things didn't work.

477
00:22:18,380 --> 00:22:20,360
So that's been both exciting to see,

478
00:22:20,360 --> 00:22:23,372
and also sometimes, I think, for many of us a bit overwhelming,

479
00:22:23,372 --> 00:22:25,580
because you're like, oh, gosh, the problems I thought

480
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:27,540
were problems aren't problems anymore.

481
00:22:27,540 --> 00:22:29,580
So what are the problems?

482
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:33,180
Of course, I work in the world of--

483
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:36,710
I work a lot on biodiversity loss

484
00:22:36,710 --> 00:22:38,423
at a global scale using machine learning.

485
00:22:38,423 --> 00:22:40,590
And I would say that biodiversity loss is definitely

486
00:22:40,590 --> 00:22:43,048
still a problem, and one that machine learning is sometimes

487
00:22:43,048 --> 00:22:46,630
helping, but often also hurting, because the carbon

488
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:52,030
costs of actually training these models is significant.

489
00:22:52,030 --> 00:22:56,250
But all of these massive things-- massive

490
00:22:56,250 --> 00:22:57,310
isn't necessary.

491
00:22:57,310 --> 00:22:59,950
So things like Stable Diffusion are actually pretty lightweight

492
00:22:59,950 --> 00:23:01,890
and were incredibly impactful.

493
00:23:01,890 --> 00:23:06,150
So things don't always need to be big to be good.

494
00:23:06,150 --> 00:23:08,390
And then I think another really important component

495
00:23:08,390 --> 00:23:11,510
of deep learning today is the idea that it is primarily

496
00:23:11,510 --> 00:23:13,590
an open source community where we have

497
00:23:13,590 --> 00:23:15,990
what we call modular reuse.

498
00:23:15,990 --> 00:23:19,750
So people take weights that were trained by one person with one

499
00:23:19,750 --> 00:23:20,370
architecture.

500
00:23:20,370 --> 00:23:22,870
They might take that and use that entire thing as a module

501
00:23:22,870 --> 00:23:24,990
within another, larger system that

502
00:23:24,990 --> 00:23:27,230
has many different components that are being

503
00:23:27,230 --> 00:23:29,670
pulled from different things.

504
00:23:29,670 --> 00:23:32,447
Now, I think that this open source perspective is really

505
00:23:32,447 --> 00:23:35,030
something that's driven a lot of progress in machine learning.

506
00:23:35,030 --> 00:23:36,447
But also one of the things that we

507
00:23:36,447 --> 00:23:38,850
have been seeing in the last few years

508
00:23:38,850 --> 00:23:42,650
is that things are less and less likely to be open source.

509
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:45,130
You might have an API, but you aren't necessarily

510
00:23:45,130 --> 00:23:47,930
going to get the weights and the architecture for something

511
00:23:47,930 --> 00:23:50,770
like GPT 4.0.

512
00:23:50,770 --> 00:23:53,410
So for the rest of the lecture, this

513
00:23:53,410 --> 00:23:55,570
is the signposting I'm going to use.

514
00:23:55,570 --> 00:23:58,795
Looking back, this is what we expect you've seen before.

515
00:23:58,795 --> 00:24:00,170
The green looking forward, that's

516
00:24:00,170 --> 00:24:02,170
what we're going to cover in this class.

517
00:24:02,170 --> 00:24:05,170
All right, pretty straightforward.

518
00:24:05,170 --> 00:24:07,330
I expect you have seen gradient descent

519
00:24:07,330 --> 00:24:09,970
before you come to this class.

520
00:24:09,970 --> 00:24:13,450
So gradient descent essentially is the idea

521
00:24:13,450 --> 00:24:17,938
that we're trying to optimize some cost function.

522
00:24:17,938 --> 00:24:19,730
And the way we're going to do that is we're

523
00:24:19,730 --> 00:24:24,290
finding some minimum over a set of all of our training data,

524
00:24:24,290 --> 00:24:27,650
some minimum set of parameters, where then the loss is actually

525
00:24:27,650 --> 00:24:30,530
minimized for all of those different data points.

526
00:24:30,530 --> 00:24:33,220
So what this actually looks like in practice,

527
00:24:33,220 --> 00:24:35,300
you're trying to find some theta star, which

528
00:24:35,300 --> 00:24:37,620
is like your optimal set of model parameters

529
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:39,320
that minimize your cost function.

530
00:24:39,320 --> 00:24:41,620
And so maybe you start somewhere random,

531
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:44,900
and then you calculate your gradient.

532
00:24:44,900 --> 00:24:47,820
Now you update the weights of that model

533
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,840
in the direction of the gradient, and you step through,

534
00:24:50,840 --> 00:24:53,180
et cetera, et cetera.

535
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:56,620
And I think many of you have probably seen this before,

536
00:24:56,620 --> 00:24:59,020
and we'll talk in an upcoming lecture,

537
00:24:59,020 --> 00:25:01,020
cover some of the different dimensions of things

538
00:25:01,020 --> 00:25:03,940
like stochastic gradient descent, et cetera.

539
00:25:03,940 --> 00:25:08,260
But we're assuming that you've seen this idea before,

540
00:25:08,260 --> 00:25:12,060
probably in intro to ML lecture, for example.

541
00:25:12,060 --> 00:25:15,180
Covering in this class, we'll go into a little bit more detail,

542
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:17,060
backprop and specifically this idea

543
00:25:17,060 --> 00:25:19,540
of differential programming.

544
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:21,980
So what does it actually look like to build programming

545
00:25:21,980 --> 00:25:26,580
languages that are structured around the idea of optimality

546
00:25:26,580 --> 00:25:29,780
and optimizing through gradient descent for different components

547
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:32,030
of that architecture?

548
00:25:32,030 --> 00:25:33,910
And I think one of the things that's fun

549
00:25:33,910 --> 00:25:37,470
is thinking about what are you actually optimizing for,

550
00:25:37,470 --> 00:25:42,150
and what are you pushing the gradients through to?

551
00:25:42,150 --> 00:25:47,910
So here, one iteration of gradient descent

552
00:25:47,910 --> 00:25:50,590
is essentially something like this,

553
00:25:50,590 --> 00:25:54,910
and we have some learning rate.

554
00:25:54,910 --> 00:25:57,590
And all of that, we're going to go into in a lot more detail

555
00:25:57,590 --> 00:26:00,430
next Tuesday where we're going to be talking about,

556
00:26:00,430 --> 00:26:02,230
where does this actually come through,

557
00:26:02,230 --> 00:26:05,630
how do we think about what some of these choices are,

558
00:26:05,630 --> 00:26:08,950
and how we actually build out the learning

559
00:26:08,950 --> 00:26:11,590
structure for backprop.

560
00:26:11,590 --> 00:26:13,190
I do expect that you've seen things

561
00:26:13,190 --> 00:26:16,110
like multilayer perceptrons and nonlinearities, things

562
00:26:16,110 --> 00:26:18,430
like ReLUs, before.

563
00:26:18,430 --> 00:26:21,710
So computational neural network is often some form of vector

564
00:26:21,710 --> 00:26:23,670
in, vector out.

565
00:26:23,670 --> 00:26:25,990
And this arrow that goes from vector in to vector

566
00:26:25,990 --> 00:26:27,870
out is a lot of what we're going to be

567
00:26:27,870 --> 00:26:30,370
talking about in this class.

568
00:26:30,370 --> 00:26:33,570
What computation does that cover?

569
00:26:33,570 --> 00:26:35,070
How does that actually build out?

570
00:26:35,070 --> 00:26:36,695
So one cool thing about neural networks

571
00:26:36,695 --> 00:26:40,490
is that they often are reusing the same simple computational

572
00:26:40,490 --> 00:26:42,610
units over and over again.

573
00:26:42,610 --> 00:26:46,250
So there's often a lot of the same kinds of computation

574
00:26:46,250 --> 00:26:49,157
that are being repeated and reused.

575
00:26:49,157 --> 00:26:51,490
And what these actually look like, these building blocks

576
00:26:51,490 --> 00:26:54,130
that are being used over and over again,

577
00:26:54,130 --> 00:26:57,610
they're often some combination of a linear layer.

578
00:26:57,610 --> 00:27:00,250
So here-- and we will be sticking with this type

579
00:27:00,250 --> 00:27:02,570
of notation for the rest of the class--

580
00:27:02,570 --> 00:27:05,450
what we call z sub j, so some component of this output

581
00:27:05,450 --> 00:27:08,570
representation in that linear layer,

582
00:27:08,570 --> 00:27:13,490
that would be a sum over i of some weight times each

583
00:27:13,490 --> 00:27:18,570
of those input components-- basically, pretty simple.

584
00:27:18,570 --> 00:27:21,507
And that can also be written essentially this way, where

585
00:27:21,507 --> 00:27:23,090
you have some set of weights, and then

586
00:27:23,090 --> 00:27:25,110
you also incorporate a bias term.

587
00:27:25,110 --> 00:27:27,210
And this is a term that does not actually

588
00:27:27,210 --> 00:27:29,160
take in any of those input components.

589
00:27:29,160 --> 00:27:31,500
It's just a bias.

590
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:34,780
And that can also be written in a vectorized notation, which

591
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:36,580
makes everything much more simple.

592
00:27:36,580 --> 00:27:41,140
So again, just with a focus on the notation, that output unit z

593
00:27:41,140 --> 00:27:46,100
sub j is x, that input vector, transposed times

594
00:27:46,100 --> 00:27:49,220
w sub j, so the set of weights that correspond to that output

595
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,580
component and that bias term.

596
00:27:52,580 --> 00:27:54,702
And then what we consider theta-- so

597
00:27:54,702 --> 00:27:56,660
that's all the parameters of the model-- that's

598
00:27:56,660 --> 00:28:01,140
the set of all the weight terms and all the bias terms.

599
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:02,748
So what actually makes a neural net

600
00:28:02,748 --> 00:28:04,540
a neural net is the fact that it's not just

601
00:28:04,540 --> 00:28:06,620
linear combinations of inputs.

602
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:10,860
You also have some sort of nonlinear function

603
00:28:10,860 --> 00:28:12,920
that is mapping your outputs.

604
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:15,180
And that's something like g sub z.

605
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:16,620
And in this case, that's something

606
00:28:16,620 --> 00:28:18,482
like a pointwise non-linearity.

607
00:28:18,482 --> 00:28:20,940
So that's an important dimension that these non-linearities

608
00:28:20,940 --> 00:28:23,140
are usually pointwise.

609
00:28:23,140 --> 00:28:25,860
So one possible non-linearity would be this one.

610
00:28:25,860 --> 00:28:29,990
g sub z would be 1 if z is greater than 0, or 0 otherwise.

611
00:28:29,990 --> 00:28:32,650
Is this a good choice of non-linearity for a neural net?

612
00:28:32,650 --> 00:28:33,357
AUDIENCE: No.

613
00:28:33,357 --> 00:28:34,190
SARA BEERY: Why not?

614
00:28:34,190 --> 00:28:36,030
AUDIENCE: It's not differentiable?

615
00:28:36,030 --> 00:28:39,070
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so it's not differentiable, exactly.

616
00:28:39,070 --> 00:28:43,890
And why is the fact that it's not differentiable not good?

617
00:28:43,890 --> 00:28:44,390
Yeah.

618
00:28:44,390 --> 00:28:47,350
AUDIENCE: Because it hinders backpropagation.

619
00:28:47,350 --> 00:28:49,150
SARA BEERY: It hinders backpropagation.

620
00:28:49,150 --> 00:28:51,070
Why?

621
00:28:51,070 --> 00:28:53,470
AUDIENCE: It makes the [INAUDIBLE] set

622
00:28:53,470 --> 00:28:55,430
harder [INAUDIBLE].

623
00:28:55,430 --> 00:28:57,210
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so the directionality.

624
00:28:57,210 --> 00:29:01,750
If you're anywhere on this graph,

625
00:29:01,750 --> 00:29:04,550
you don't know which way to go, essentially.

626
00:29:04,550 --> 00:29:05,950
The gradient is zero.

627
00:29:05,950 --> 00:29:08,610
So you can try to take a gradient ascent step.

628
00:29:08,610 --> 00:29:13,430
You're not going to move because the gradient is zero, exactly.

629
00:29:13,430 --> 00:29:18,870
So this roughly is called a perceptron, this linear input

630
00:29:18,870 --> 00:29:22,270
plus a pointwise non-linearity.

631
00:29:22,270 --> 00:29:26,970
And you actually can arguably do linear classification

632
00:29:26,970 --> 00:29:27,950
with a perceptron.

633
00:29:27,950 --> 00:29:30,730
So if you take a really simple version

634
00:29:30,730 --> 00:29:34,330
of this where your input is just two-dimensional, x1, x2,

635
00:29:34,330 --> 00:29:37,610
and you have some w1/w2 learned weight terms that you use

636
00:29:37,610 --> 00:29:39,910
to combine to get z, which is your hidden unit,

637
00:29:39,910 --> 00:29:43,770
and then you map that via a function to y,

638
00:29:43,770 --> 00:29:46,770
you then define z as x transpose w plus b.

639
00:29:46,770 --> 00:29:51,170
And y is now some function g of z.

640
00:29:51,170 --> 00:29:54,570
What this looks like for any set of values

641
00:29:54,570 --> 00:29:56,970
will be something like this, where you essentially

642
00:29:56,970 --> 00:29:58,610
have almost a ramp.

643
00:29:58,610 --> 00:30:04,290
It's a clear and standard gradient across.

644
00:30:04,290 --> 00:30:06,150
Why would it look like this?

645
00:30:06,150 --> 00:30:10,750
So why is it smooth and reasonably straight?

646
00:30:14,330 --> 00:30:17,890
I maybe didn't pose that question super clearly.

647
00:30:17,890 --> 00:30:21,330
So essentially, the reason that mapping it

648
00:30:21,330 --> 00:30:24,330
looks like this is because it's a plane.

649
00:30:24,330 --> 00:30:28,940
So any two-dimensional version of this

650
00:30:28,940 --> 00:30:33,780
is going to be a plane with some orientation.

651
00:30:33,780 --> 00:30:36,200
And then very straightforward, actually,

652
00:30:36,200 --> 00:30:38,140
to turn that into a linear classifier,

653
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:39,580
because all you're doing is taking

654
00:30:39,580 --> 00:30:41,820
some threshold on that plane.

655
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:43,900
So even a single-layer neural network

656
00:30:43,900 --> 00:30:46,040
can perform linear classification

657
00:30:46,040 --> 00:30:47,580
because now this threshold, that's

658
00:30:47,580 --> 00:30:50,320
basically what that stepwise non-linearity is giving you.

659
00:30:50,320 --> 00:30:55,180
It's giving you something that is thresholded at some value.

660
00:30:55,180 --> 00:30:57,160
And so even with something quite simple,

661
00:30:57,160 --> 00:30:59,060
you can do classification.

662
00:30:59,060 --> 00:31:02,655
Now, that's assuming that your data is linearly separable.

663
00:31:02,655 --> 00:31:04,280
So if you have training data like this,

664
00:31:04,280 --> 00:31:08,340
which is linearly separable, and now you want to find some

665
00:31:08,340 --> 00:31:12,020
optimal set of weights and biases that will minimize some

666
00:31:12,020 --> 00:31:16,500
loss term where now, again, this loss is the difference between

667
00:31:16,500 --> 00:31:19,940
the true value-- so here, the true value is 1 or 0--

668
00:31:19,940 --> 00:31:23,620
and the functional mapping of the inputs to something

669
00:31:23,620 --> 00:31:25,128
like that value.

670
00:31:25,128 --> 00:31:26,670
If you have something like this, this

671
00:31:26,670 --> 00:31:28,150
is what we'd call a bad fit.

672
00:31:28,150 --> 00:31:31,030
You have 7 misclassifications.

673
00:31:31,030 --> 00:31:33,270
You're wrong about more things than you're right.

674
00:31:33,270 --> 00:31:35,510
And all of those red values, those

675
00:31:35,510 --> 00:31:38,950
are going to give you a high value in your loss function.

676
00:31:38,950 --> 00:31:42,550
Or you could find something that's like an OK fit,

677
00:31:42,550 --> 00:31:45,090
less misclassifications.

678
00:31:45,090 --> 00:31:48,030
And maybe this is something that your model updates to

679
00:31:48,030 --> 00:31:50,110
after a single gradient step.

680
00:31:50,110 --> 00:31:53,070
Your decision surface is linear, and now you're fiddling around

681
00:31:53,070 --> 00:31:56,990
with what that classifier is--

682
00:31:56,990 --> 00:32:00,910
basically, which values of weights and biases you need.

683
00:32:00,910 --> 00:32:02,427
And then maybe after a few steps,

684
00:32:02,427 --> 00:32:04,510
you would find something that is a good fit, where

685
00:32:04,510 --> 00:32:07,190
you're not actually misclassifying anything.

686
00:32:07,190 --> 00:32:09,590
So it is possible to do classification

687
00:32:09,590 --> 00:32:12,210
of linear things, linearly separable things,

688
00:32:12,210 --> 00:32:14,310
even with something like a perceptron that's

689
00:32:14,310 --> 00:32:16,670
doing these really simple thresholding-based

690
00:32:16,670 --> 00:32:19,270
non-linearities.

691
00:32:19,270 --> 00:32:21,870
But like we said, this actually would

692
00:32:21,870 --> 00:32:24,970
be really difficult to learn.

693
00:32:24,970 --> 00:32:29,850
So instead, maybe you would try something like a tanh.

694
00:32:29,850 --> 00:32:33,930
So this is another pointwise non-linearity.

695
00:32:33,930 --> 00:32:38,610
And it was one of the earliest ones that people looked at.

696
00:32:38,610 --> 00:32:40,130
The things that are nice about this

697
00:32:40,130 --> 00:32:42,970
is it's bounded between minus 1 and 1, so none of the values

698
00:32:42,970 --> 00:32:44,747
are super huge.

699
00:32:44,747 --> 00:32:46,330
And you get saturation of the gradient

700
00:32:46,330 --> 00:32:48,070
for super large or super small inputs.

701
00:32:48,070 --> 00:32:51,550
You're not getting explosions necessarily.

702
00:32:51,550 --> 00:32:53,450
But also, if you start out somewhere that's

703
00:32:53,450 --> 00:32:55,010
really far from the center, you don't

704
00:32:55,010 --> 00:32:58,170
have a lot of training signal because we're getting close to 0

705
00:32:58,170 --> 00:32:59,730
in our gradient.

706
00:32:59,730 --> 00:33:01,730
And so those gradients do go to 0

707
00:33:01,730 --> 00:33:04,690
as we go to infinity in either direction.

708
00:33:04,690 --> 00:33:07,610
The outputs are centered at 0.

709
00:33:07,610 --> 00:33:12,370
So the tanh of z can also be written as 2 times the sigmoid

710
00:33:12,370 --> 00:33:13,890
of 2z minus 1.

711
00:33:13,890 --> 00:33:16,450
So let's look at what that sigmoid function looks like.

712
00:33:16,450 --> 00:33:19,810
So in this case, this is a sigmoid, 1 over 1

713
00:33:19,810 --> 00:33:25,090
plus e to the minus h or e to the minus--

714
00:33:25,090 --> 00:33:26,400
the notation is wrong.

715
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:28,500
It should be minus z.

716
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:31,420
This can be interpreted as the firing rate of a neuron.

717
00:33:31,420 --> 00:33:34,280
That was one interpretation of it initially.

718
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:38,580
It's bounded between 0 and 1, so it's not ever negative.

719
00:33:38,580 --> 00:33:40,620
Again, you have saturation of that gradient

720
00:33:40,620 --> 00:33:43,460
for super large or super small inputs.

721
00:33:43,460 --> 00:33:46,380
And all those gradients go to 0.

722
00:33:46,380 --> 00:33:48,758
And the outputs themselves are centered around 0.5.

723
00:33:48,758 --> 00:33:50,300
So this is kind of poor conditioning.

724
00:33:50,300 --> 00:33:52,460
It's slightly biased.

725
00:33:52,460 --> 00:33:55,140
In practice, we don't actually use this.

726
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:57,660
So instead, in practice, what we often

727
00:33:57,660 --> 00:33:59,660
use is something called a rectified linear unit,

728
00:33:59,660 --> 00:34:01,060
or a ReLU.

729
00:34:01,060 --> 00:34:05,620
And this is just the max of 0 and z.

730
00:34:05,620 --> 00:34:08,760
And this is unbounded on the positive side,

731
00:34:08,760 --> 00:34:12,219
so it can go all the way to infinity, which can result in,

732
00:34:12,219 --> 00:34:14,580
for example, sometimes things like exploding gradients,

733
00:34:14,580 --> 00:34:16,460
because the values can get very large.

734
00:34:16,460 --> 00:34:18,860
But it's super efficient to implement.

735
00:34:18,860 --> 00:34:23,070
Essentially, the derivatives are just that step function.

736
00:34:23,070 --> 00:34:25,860
And it also seems to help speed up convergence.

737
00:34:25,860 --> 00:34:28,110
So in that Krizhevsky paper, they showed that they got

738
00:34:28,110 --> 00:34:32,469
something like a 6x speed-up using a ReLU over using

739
00:34:32,469 --> 00:34:34,830
something like a tanh in terms of converging that model

740
00:34:34,830 --> 00:34:36,110
and learning.

741
00:34:36,110 --> 00:34:39,050
The drawback is that if you're strongly in the negative region,

742
00:34:39,050 --> 00:34:41,429
the unit's what we call dead.

743
00:34:41,429 --> 00:34:44,429
There is no gradient, and so you essentially

744
00:34:44,429 --> 00:34:46,750
have this gradient collapse, where

745
00:34:46,750 --> 00:34:51,431
you can no longer learn from that component of the vector.

746
00:34:51,431 --> 00:34:53,389
But this is our default choice, and it's widely

747
00:34:53,389 --> 00:34:54,710
used in current models.

748
00:34:54,710 --> 00:34:56,969
There's lots of slight tweaks to this,

749
00:34:56,969 --> 00:34:59,870
but this is really, really common.

750
00:34:59,870 --> 00:35:02,910
So then the last thing I want to talk about in this section

751
00:35:02,910 --> 00:35:04,630
is stacking layers.

752
00:35:04,630 --> 00:35:06,590
So we talked about a single version

753
00:35:06,590 --> 00:35:09,590
of this, where you have your linear projection,

754
00:35:09,590 --> 00:35:11,278
and then you have a non-linearity.

755
00:35:11,278 --> 00:35:13,070
But now you can take that, and you can just

756
00:35:13,070 --> 00:35:14,910
stack another one on top of it.

757
00:35:14,910 --> 00:35:18,675
So now both z and h are hidden units.

758
00:35:18,675 --> 00:35:20,550
They're somewhere in the middle of the model.

759
00:35:20,550 --> 00:35:23,010
We don't necessarily-- they're not seen

760
00:35:23,010 --> 00:35:25,730
at the inputs or the outputs.

761
00:35:25,730 --> 00:35:27,210
And essentially, then, you can also

762
00:35:27,210 --> 00:35:28,850
remember that all of these things

763
00:35:28,850 --> 00:35:32,450
are stacked on top of each other, almost in parallel

764
00:35:32,450 --> 00:35:34,610
as well, because all of these inputs are mapping

765
00:35:34,610 --> 00:35:37,090
to the different components of the output vector.

766
00:35:37,090 --> 00:35:40,490
And so you have weights for all of those different dimensions.

767
00:35:40,490 --> 00:35:45,010
So this means that h, our output of the first layer,

768
00:35:45,010 --> 00:35:48,370
is going to be g, that pointwise non-linearity, applied

769
00:35:48,370 --> 00:35:54,050
to now instead of vectorized w transpose x sub j to give us

770
00:35:54,050 --> 00:35:57,370
the individual j-th component, you can actually just think

771
00:35:57,370 --> 00:35:59,910
of this now as a matrix multiplied by a vector,

772
00:35:59,910 --> 00:36:02,810
all of the weights for all of those different dimensions

773
00:36:02,810 --> 00:36:06,810
multiplied by the vector x plus the vector of all your biases,

774
00:36:06,810 --> 00:36:07,810
b1.

775
00:36:07,810 --> 00:36:10,690
So now you have this cleaner notation.

776
00:36:10,690 --> 00:36:15,450
And then now y is again g, that same pointwise non-linearity

777
00:36:15,450 --> 00:36:20,060
applied on top of your outputs of your first thing,

778
00:36:20,060 --> 00:36:23,780
but now with h, multiplied by that vector of all

779
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:26,060
your second-layer weights and added

780
00:36:26,060 --> 00:36:28,020
with your second-layer bias term.

781
00:36:28,020 --> 00:36:31,175
And so now, your set theta, your model representation,

782
00:36:31,175 --> 00:36:33,300
is the set of all of the different weights from all

783
00:36:33,300 --> 00:36:35,620
the different layers, as well as all the different biases in all

784
00:36:35,620 --> 00:36:36,495
the different layers.

785
00:36:36,495 --> 00:36:37,780
Oh, thank you.

786
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:38,280
Oh, yeah.

787
00:36:38,280 --> 00:36:39,198
Thank you.

788
00:36:39,198 --> 00:36:40,740
AUDIENCE: Based off your application,

789
00:36:40,740 --> 00:36:42,600
how do you choose your activation function?

790
00:36:42,600 --> 00:36:44,740
What are the parameters of what might be considered

791
00:36:44,740 --> 00:36:49,380
a good model or, I guess, a good activation function

792
00:36:49,380 --> 00:36:52,820
based off of what you want to use your model for?

793
00:36:52,820 --> 00:36:54,760
SARA BEERY: So you're asking, in practice,

794
00:36:54,760 --> 00:36:58,420
are there any best practices for choosing the non-linearity based

795
00:36:58,420 --> 00:37:02,100
on different types of data.

796
00:37:02,100 --> 00:37:03,940
I think in the literature, there's maybe--

797
00:37:03,940 --> 00:37:06,120
you can see it coming out.

798
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:12,220
But often, people really do use ReLU quite a lot.

799
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:17,150
But I wouldn't say that there's any great general rules of thumb

800
00:37:17,150 --> 00:37:17,710
there.

801
00:37:17,710 --> 00:37:25,690
And often-- yeah.

802
00:37:25,690 --> 00:37:27,190
I would say there's different things

803
00:37:27,190 --> 00:37:30,650
that people have converged upon based on experimental success.

804
00:37:30,650 --> 00:37:33,715
So you'll see in the literature like, oh, yeah,

805
00:37:33,715 --> 00:37:35,590
everything in this line of work on these data

806
00:37:35,590 --> 00:37:38,350
sets with these things, they're always using

807
00:37:38,350 --> 00:37:40,650
one or the other component.

808
00:37:40,650 --> 00:37:42,250
And then people build on that.

809
00:37:42,250 --> 00:37:44,690
Sometimes that's called grad student gradient descent

810
00:37:44,690 --> 00:37:47,310
[LAUGHS] because you're essentially

811
00:37:47,310 --> 00:37:49,790
writing research papers and learning from those research

812
00:37:49,790 --> 00:37:53,110
papers what parameters in terms of things like your activation

813
00:37:53,110 --> 00:37:54,170
functions make sense.

814
00:37:54,170 --> 00:37:57,830
But yeah, I don't know that I have great rules of thumb

815
00:37:57,830 --> 00:37:59,470
of, oh, for this type of application,

816
00:37:59,470 --> 00:38:02,790
you should always use this or that.

817
00:38:02,790 --> 00:38:03,950
Do you?

818
00:38:03,950 --> 00:38:05,710
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's not a hard science.

819
00:38:05,710 --> 00:38:06,608
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

820
00:38:06,608 --> 00:38:08,150
AUDIENCE: But it's usually about what

821
00:38:08,150 --> 00:38:10,910
makes the network more trainable rather than trying to model

822
00:38:10,910 --> 00:38:12,550
a certain kind of data.

823
00:38:12,550 --> 00:38:14,630
There are a couple of examples, like using

824
00:38:14,630 --> 00:38:18,210
a sinusoidal non-linearity to pick out some for you,

825
00:38:18,210 --> 00:38:19,130
if you'd like.

826
00:38:19,130 --> 00:38:21,490
There are a few examples that do but usually not.

827
00:38:21,490 --> 00:38:25,450
And [INAUDIBLE] does deep learning has a great answer

828
00:38:25,450 --> 00:38:26,262
to that question.

829
00:38:26,262 --> 00:38:26,970
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

830
00:38:26,970 --> 00:38:28,370
So what Jeremy was saying is basically,

831
00:38:28,370 --> 00:38:29,470
it's not a hard science.

832
00:38:29,470 --> 00:38:31,450
We don't have theoretical guarantees

833
00:38:31,450 --> 00:38:34,250
of this is what you should do.

834
00:38:34,250 --> 00:38:36,190
But in practice, there are-- yeah,

835
00:38:36,190 --> 00:38:38,325
there's counterexamples to that.

836
00:38:38,325 --> 00:38:40,450
So if you know, for example, that your features are

837
00:38:40,450 --> 00:38:43,370
sinusoidal, it maybe makes sense to try to pick out

838
00:38:43,370 --> 00:38:44,630
Fourier representations.

839
00:38:44,630 --> 00:38:46,130
You're working in the Fourier space,

840
00:38:46,130 --> 00:38:49,530
then maybe you should use a sinusoidal activation.

841
00:38:49,530 --> 00:38:52,710
Anyway, but yes, I wish that we better understood it.

842
00:38:52,710 --> 00:38:55,650
And that's, I think, why we need a lot more research

843
00:38:55,650 --> 00:38:57,610
on the theoretical side of understanding,

844
00:38:57,610 --> 00:39:02,210
how do we actually choose these things appropriately

845
00:39:02,210 --> 00:39:04,570
instead of maybe learning everything from scratch

846
00:39:04,570 --> 00:39:07,710
or doing it experimentally, burning all those trees?

847
00:39:10,590 --> 00:39:11,090
Cool.

848
00:39:11,090 --> 00:39:12,730
So the parameters of our model are now

849
00:39:12,730 --> 00:39:14,188
all the different layers of weights

850
00:39:14,188 --> 00:39:17,020
and all the different layers of biases.

851
00:39:17,020 --> 00:39:20,060
And so like we said, you can do linear classification

852
00:39:20,060 --> 00:39:21,040
with a perceptron.

853
00:39:21,040 --> 00:39:23,020
But the fun thing is-- oops--

854
00:39:23,020 --> 00:39:25,660
you can do non-linear classification with a deep net,

855
00:39:25,660 --> 00:39:29,380
even if that deep net is just a two-layer perceptron.

856
00:39:29,380 --> 00:39:33,340
So here, now we've built a double-layer model, still

857
00:39:33,340 --> 00:39:34,480
only in two dimensions.

858
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:38,020
So now we have mapped through essentially just

859
00:39:38,020 --> 00:39:40,500
this simple non-linearity in between these two

860
00:39:40,500 --> 00:39:42,140
different layers.

861
00:39:42,140 --> 00:39:44,020
Now if we look at what this looks like,

862
00:39:44,020 --> 00:39:48,500
that first layer is giving us one kind of ramp.

863
00:39:48,500 --> 00:39:50,540
The second layer gives us a different ramp

864
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:52,340
because it's a different linear model.

865
00:39:52,340 --> 00:39:55,380
And now if you do this non-linear combination of them,

866
00:39:55,380 --> 00:39:58,740
essentially what you get out is you can actually map--

867
00:39:58,740 --> 00:40:01,780
it's almost like think about the intersection of one ramp

868
00:40:01,780 --> 00:40:03,140
with another ramp.

869
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:07,460
You get a kind of triangular, almost a pyramidal component

870
00:40:07,460 --> 00:40:10,520
that will be higher than everything else.

871
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:13,600
And so now you can still take a simple threshold of that,

872
00:40:13,600 --> 00:40:16,237
and now you can get out a non-linear classification.

873
00:40:16,237 --> 00:40:18,820
And, of course, you can expand this into many more dimensions.

874
00:40:18,820 --> 00:40:21,680
But this is, I think, some nice, simple intuition building

875
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:25,640
as to why you start getting non-linear models when you're

876
00:40:25,640 --> 00:40:30,600
taking linear combinations, but with these non-linearities.

877
00:40:30,600 --> 00:40:31,320
Cool.

878
00:40:31,320 --> 00:40:34,520
So another-- so we expect that you've seen a lot of that

879
00:40:34,520 --> 00:40:35,400
before.

880
00:40:35,400 --> 00:40:37,062
But moving forward, one of the things

881
00:40:37,062 --> 00:40:38,520
we are going to cover in this class

882
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:41,120
is what we can approximate.

883
00:40:41,120 --> 00:40:44,080
So what do we know about what is possible to

884
00:40:44,080 --> 00:40:47,000
approximate with deep learning?

885
00:40:47,000 --> 00:40:50,280
So one dimension of that is representational power.

886
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:52,000
So you have a single layer.

887
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:55,800
You can do any linear decision surface.

888
00:40:55,800 --> 00:40:57,800
If you have 2-plus layers, in theory,

889
00:40:57,800 --> 00:41:01,280
you can actually represent any function.

890
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:03,280
And this is assuming, of course, that you

891
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:05,040
have some non-trivial non-linearity

892
00:41:05,040 --> 00:41:06,898
between those layers because if you don't

893
00:41:06,898 --> 00:41:08,440
have that non-linearity, it turns out

894
00:41:08,440 --> 00:41:11,520
that a linear layer and another linear layer, this

895
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:13,130
is just a linear combination.

896
00:41:13,130 --> 00:41:14,210
It's still linear.

897
00:41:14,210 --> 00:41:16,130
So you need that non-linearity.

898
00:41:16,130 --> 00:41:18,750
But doing that, you can actually approximate anything.

899
00:41:18,750 --> 00:41:20,210
And one way to think about it is,

900
00:41:20,210 --> 00:41:24,970
given some amount of capacity, some number

901
00:41:24,970 --> 00:41:28,350
of different dimensions that you can do,

902
00:41:28,350 --> 00:41:30,470
you can actually approximate any function.

903
00:41:30,470 --> 00:41:35,390
This is a rough, hand-wavy intuition thing.

904
00:41:35,390 --> 00:41:44,843
But as you're shrinking the size of these different vertical

905
00:41:44,843 --> 00:41:47,010
things, you're getting something like a Riemann sum.

906
00:41:47,010 --> 00:41:48,610
You can approximate any integral as long

907
00:41:48,610 --> 00:41:50,110
as you have enough capacity in terms

908
00:41:50,110 --> 00:41:52,890
of the number of small things that you're building up.

909
00:41:52,890 --> 00:41:57,450
So this gets at basically one of the issues, which is efficiency.

910
00:41:57,450 --> 00:41:59,130
So in theory, you could approximate

911
00:41:59,130 --> 00:42:03,490
any complicated function with a sufficiently wide

912
00:42:03,490 --> 00:42:04,550
two-layer network.

913
00:42:04,550 --> 00:42:06,970
So this means not two dimensions, maybe thousands

914
00:42:06,970 --> 00:42:09,330
or millions of dimensions.

915
00:42:09,330 --> 00:42:13,660
But in practice, that's actually very inefficient.

916
00:42:13,660 --> 00:42:16,420
So a narrow deep model, maybe something

917
00:42:16,420 --> 00:42:19,380
that only has much fewer dimensions in terms

918
00:42:19,380 --> 00:42:22,360
of the size of each vector or each input and output,

919
00:42:22,360 --> 00:42:25,420
but now many, many, many more stacked layers,

920
00:42:25,420 --> 00:42:27,760
you might be able to, with a lot less parameters,

921
00:42:27,760 --> 00:42:30,540
approximate the same complex function.

922
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:32,080
In practice, we do find that's true.

923
00:42:32,080 --> 00:42:34,137
More layers helps.

924
00:42:34,137 --> 00:42:36,220
And we'll get into this a little bit in lecture 3,

925
00:42:36,220 --> 00:42:39,060
which is about approximation theory, the theory of what

926
00:42:39,060 --> 00:42:41,482
it is possible to approximate.

927
00:42:41,482 --> 00:42:42,940
And then the other thing that we're

928
00:42:42,940 --> 00:42:45,420
going to cover in this class is architectures.

929
00:42:45,420 --> 00:42:47,640
So when we talk about deep networks,

930
00:42:47,640 --> 00:42:50,420
really, often, we're talking about something that's roughly

931
00:42:50,420 --> 00:42:54,520
like this, some cascade of repeated simple computations,

932
00:42:54,520 --> 00:42:56,860
each of these linear and then linear models

933
00:42:56,860 --> 00:42:58,740
with a non-linearity.

934
00:42:58,740 --> 00:43:00,540
And then with every increasing layer,

935
00:43:00,540 --> 00:43:03,120
we're getting more abstracted representations.

936
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:05,740
You're getting a higher capacity model.

937
00:43:05,740 --> 00:43:08,380
And this means it can form more complex and abstract

938
00:43:08,380 --> 00:43:09,620
calculations.

939
00:43:09,620 --> 00:43:13,660
Whereas a shallow network, if it's not sufficiently wide,

940
00:43:13,660 --> 00:43:16,800
it could maybe only efficiently compute simple things

941
00:43:16,800 --> 00:43:19,135
like maybe finding edges, a deep network

942
00:43:19,135 --> 00:43:20,760
could efficiently compute harder things

943
00:43:20,760 --> 00:43:24,640
like recognizing this clownfish or translating English

944
00:43:24,640 --> 00:43:27,080
to Chinese.

945
00:43:27,080 --> 00:43:29,760
And so this classification of complex things,

946
00:43:29,760 --> 00:43:31,360
essentially, your whole function f

947
00:43:31,360 --> 00:43:36,440
of x is now this stacked or almost chain-like

948
00:43:36,440 --> 00:43:39,280
mapping from your final layer, which is then

949
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:42,400
a functional mapping of your previous layer,

950
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:46,160
and in and in and in and in, all the way back to that input.

951
00:43:46,160 --> 00:43:47,720
And there's a lot of different ways

952
00:43:47,720 --> 00:43:49,680
that we've learned to build these mappings

953
00:43:49,680 --> 00:43:52,080
for different types of problems and based

954
00:43:52,080 --> 00:43:54,260
on the structure of the data we're trying to model.

955
00:43:54,260 --> 00:43:55,260
And we'll get into that.

956
00:43:55,260 --> 00:43:58,540
We'll talk about CNNs, GNNs, transformers, and RNNs,

957
00:43:58,540 --> 00:44:01,440
so convolutional neural networks, graph neural networks,

958
00:44:01,440 --> 00:44:05,182
transformers, and recurrent neural networks.

959
00:44:05,182 --> 00:44:06,640
And then the other thing that we're

960
00:44:06,640 --> 00:44:08,265
going to be talking about in this class

961
00:44:08,265 --> 00:44:12,250
is when and why we can generalize.

962
00:44:12,250 --> 00:44:13,950
So why do deep nets generalize?

963
00:44:13,950 --> 00:44:15,930
Essentially, deep nets have so many parameters,

964
00:44:15,930 --> 00:44:18,190
they could just act like lookup tables.

965
00:44:18,190 --> 00:44:20,350
They would just regurgitate the training data.

966
00:44:20,350 --> 00:44:23,070
But instead, they seem to learn rules that generalize.

967
00:44:23,070 --> 00:44:25,110
And this actually defies classical theory.

968
00:44:25,110 --> 00:44:27,850
So the classical theory basically

969
00:44:27,850 --> 00:44:31,710
says that if your model is overparameterized,

970
00:44:31,710 --> 00:44:33,030
it will overfit.

971
00:44:33,030 --> 00:44:37,570
There's this capacity versus risk or error curve

972
00:44:37,570 --> 00:44:40,070
where if you don't have enough capacity,

973
00:44:40,070 --> 00:44:41,770
you're going to underfit the model.

974
00:44:41,770 --> 00:44:44,810
And then with too much capacity, you're going to overfit.

975
00:44:44,810 --> 00:44:47,570
But in practice-- and this is from this paper "Double

976
00:44:47,570 --> 00:44:48,850
Descent"--

977
00:44:48,850 --> 00:44:53,610
what we see is that in this modern interpolating regime,

978
00:44:53,610 --> 00:44:56,510
we go past that point of being overparameterized,

979
00:44:56,510 --> 00:44:59,330
and we're actually then able to actually pass

980
00:44:59,330 --> 00:45:01,450
some interpolation threshold or ability

981
00:45:01,450 --> 00:45:03,090
to have enough capacity in the model

982
00:45:03,090 --> 00:45:05,270
to interpolate between the data points.

983
00:45:05,270 --> 00:45:08,460
We're actually able to then potentially get even better,

984
00:45:08,460 --> 00:45:10,760
even though the models are massively overparameterized.

985
00:45:10,760 --> 00:45:11,355
Yeah.

986
00:45:11,355 --> 00:45:12,980
AUDIENCE: What do you mean by capacity?

987
00:45:12,980 --> 00:45:13,980
SARA BEERY: Capacity is the number

988
00:45:13,980 --> 00:45:15,440
of parameters in the model.

989
00:45:15,440 --> 00:45:17,380
So that's both the width and the depth,

990
00:45:17,380 --> 00:45:22,140
basically how many values are captured in those model weights?

991
00:45:22,140 --> 00:45:23,500
Yeah.

992
00:45:23,500 --> 00:45:26,180
Yeah.

993
00:45:26,180 --> 00:45:27,900
Yes, thank you.

994
00:45:27,900 --> 00:45:31,420
AUDIENCE: And how does that relate to the size of the data

995
00:45:31,420 --> 00:45:33,140
samples that you're passing?

996
00:45:33,140 --> 00:45:34,580
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

997
00:45:34,580 --> 00:45:38,240
So that is not a dimension on this plot,

998
00:45:38,240 --> 00:45:39,920
but it definitely is also related.

999
00:45:39,920 --> 00:45:42,980
So if you have-- but it's related

1000
00:45:42,980 --> 00:45:44,500
in a different dimension.

1001
00:45:44,500 --> 00:45:47,420
If you're learning from fewer data points,

1002
00:45:47,420 --> 00:45:51,760
it's very easy to learn to maybe overfit to those data points.

1003
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:54,760
So you could think of that honestly, in another way,

1004
00:45:54,760 --> 00:45:57,100
maybe as being a different dimension of capacity here,

1005
00:45:57,100 --> 00:45:59,720
where it's like almost the capacity of your training data.

1006
00:45:59,720 --> 00:46:02,403
But, of course, there's not a direct translation.

1007
00:46:02,403 --> 00:46:04,320
But so if you don't have enough training data,

1008
00:46:04,320 --> 00:46:06,570
you also can't learn to interpolate between those data

1009
00:46:06,570 --> 00:46:09,960
points because you don't have enough coverage of the data

1010
00:46:09,960 --> 00:46:12,255
distribution.

1011
00:46:12,255 --> 00:46:13,380
I live in that world a lot.

1012
00:46:13,380 --> 00:46:16,680
We often don't have enough data to learn

1013
00:46:16,680 --> 00:46:19,820
a general representation without a lot of handy tricks.

1014
00:46:19,820 --> 00:46:22,000
We will talk about some of those different tricks

1015
00:46:22,000 --> 00:46:23,708
in this class, some of the different ways

1016
00:46:23,708 --> 00:46:26,800
that we try to convince models not to overfit to data

1017
00:46:26,800 --> 00:46:30,560
and try to learn to generalize beyond maybe what

1018
00:46:30,560 --> 00:46:31,992
is captured in the data.

1019
00:46:31,992 --> 00:46:33,950
But I think that dimension is really important,

1020
00:46:33,950 --> 00:46:36,240
and it's one of the reasons that none of this

1021
00:46:36,240 --> 00:46:39,160
was possible until we started building big enough data sets,

1022
00:46:39,160 --> 00:46:41,360
where you had enough coverage of the space

1023
00:46:41,360 --> 00:46:43,520
that you wanted to model in your data

1024
00:46:43,520 --> 00:46:46,460
to be able to start interpolating between them.

1025
00:46:46,460 --> 00:46:46,960
Yeah.

1026
00:46:46,960 --> 00:46:48,835
AUDIENCE: Can you just clarify the difference

1027
00:46:48,835 --> 00:46:51,520
between overfitting and overparameterized?

1028
00:46:51,520 --> 00:46:54,420
SARA BEERY: Oh, yeah.

1029
00:46:54,420 --> 00:46:55,920
So, I mean, I think this paper would

1030
00:46:55,920 --> 00:46:58,220
be great to read to be able to really get into it.

1031
00:46:58,220 --> 00:47:00,720
But essentially, the idea here is

1032
00:47:00,720 --> 00:47:02,280
that in the previous literature, we

1033
00:47:02,280 --> 00:47:06,270
said that at some point, if you had too many parameters,

1034
00:47:06,270 --> 00:47:09,450
you were going to overfit and learn a model that actually

1035
00:47:09,450 --> 00:47:12,490
generalized worse because based on the amount of data that you

1036
00:47:12,490 --> 00:47:17,010
had, it was going to-- there's a classical example where you have

1037
00:47:17,010 --> 00:47:18,510
three data points.

1038
00:47:18,510 --> 00:47:23,513
And if you try to learn a 12-dimensional function,

1039
00:47:23,513 --> 00:47:25,930
it will learn something like this to fit those data points

1040
00:47:25,930 --> 00:47:26,510
perfectly.

1041
00:47:26,510 --> 00:47:28,690
But actually, it's not likely to generalize

1042
00:47:28,690 --> 00:47:32,170
because it's basically too spiky or too peaky.

1043
00:47:32,170 --> 00:47:34,250
So that's the idea of overfitting.

1044
00:47:34,250 --> 00:47:36,370
But this idea of overparameterized

1045
00:47:36,370 --> 00:47:39,450
is basically instead of saying underfitting and overfitting,

1046
00:47:39,450 --> 00:47:42,090
now we're saying that the new paradigm is underparametrized

1047
00:47:42,090 --> 00:47:43,630
and overparameterized.

1048
00:47:43,630 --> 00:47:45,410
And what they're basically saying

1049
00:47:45,410 --> 00:47:50,370
is that you maybe can't be too overparameterized,

1050
00:47:50,370 --> 00:47:53,570
though this perspective is one that doesn't

1051
00:47:53,570 --> 00:47:56,010
take into account resources.

1052
00:47:56,010 --> 00:48:00,370
So there's many, many people and places and places

1053
00:48:00,370 --> 00:48:02,970
that are at MIT, but even actually in this class,

1054
00:48:02,970 --> 00:48:05,245
where we don't have infinite compute resources.

1055
00:48:05,245 --> 00:48:06,620
So actually, what you really want

1056
00:48:06,620 --> 00:48:08,820
is some optimal point on this curve where

1057
00:48:08,820 --> 00:48:12,380
you get good performance, but you're not maybe

1058
00:48:12,380 --> 00:48:14,140
using resources that you don't have,

1059
00:48:14,140 --> 00:48:17,460
super, super large memory GPUs.

1060
00:48:17,460 --> 00:48:19,260
Yeah?

1061
00:48:19,260 --> 00:48:21,100
AUDIENCE: When you gave the example of using

1062
00:48:21,100 --> 00:48:24,860
two very wide layers versus multiple narrow ones,

1063
00:48:24,860 --> 00:48:27,680
[INAUDIBLE] there's a rule of thumb about

1064
00:48:27,680 --> 00:48:30,420
which is better for about the interpretability

1065
00:48:30,420 --> 00:48:31,700
between the two?

1066
00:48:31,700 --> 00:48:33,260
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1067
00:48:33,260 --> 00:48:35,100
OK, we're definitely going to get

1068
00:48:35,100 --> 00:48:38,260
into this a lot more in some of the later lectures,

1069
00:48:38,260 --> 00:48:44,500
these choices between breadth versus depth in models.

1070
00:48:44,500 --> 00:48:47,920
But at a very high level, your intuition,

1071
00:48:47,920 --> 00:48:50,580
I think, that you're getting at is correct.

1072
00:48:50,580 --> 00:48:54,602
If you have only two layers--

1073
00:48:54,602 --> 00:48:56,060
actually, maybe not, because if you

1074
00:48:56,060 --> 00:48:59,080
have two almost infinite parameter layers,

1075
00:48:59,080 --> 00:49:02,540
really, really high dimensional space is hard to interpret.

1076
00:49:02,540 --> 00:49:06,600
If you have lower dimensional space over many, many different

1077
00:49:06,600 --> 00:49:09,580
now stacked versions of these layers,

1078
00:49:09,580 --> 00:49:11,140
it's also hard to interpret.

1079
00:49:11,140 --> 00:49:14,840
So I would say like in ecology, for example, people often

1080
00:49:14,840 --> 00:49:16,520
still use just generative additive

1081
00:49:16,520 --> 00:49:18,575
models of a bunch of different linear things

1082
00:49:18,575 --> 00:49:20,200
because they need the interpretability,

1083
00:49:20,200 --> 00:49:22,680
or they feel like they do.

1084
00:49:22,680 --> 00:49:25,920
And anytime you get to larger numbers

1085
00:49:25,920 --> 00:49:29,000
of parameters in either dimension, that interpretability

1086
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:31,265
gets more difficult.

1087
00:49:31,265 --> 00:49:32,640
AUDIENCE: I was just going to add

1088
00:49:32,640 --> 00:49:38,440
that even in, let's say OpenAI, it's a closely guarded secret

1089
00:49:38,440 --> 00:49:40,720
what recipe of width versus depth

1090
00:49:40,720 --> 00:49:43,760
that they use in GPT whatever.

1091
00:49:43,760 --> 00:49:46,000
If you can come up with a really scientific way

1092
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:48,240
to answer that question, you could go and get

1093
00:49:48,240 --> 00:49:49,040
a job there and--

1094
00:49:49,040 --> 00:49:50,660
SARA BEERY: Make 7 figures.

1095
00:49:50,660 --> 00:49:51,600
[LAUGHS]

1096
00:49:51,600 --> 00:49:52,603
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1097
00:49:52,603 --> 00:49:54,520
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so what Jeremy is saying is,

1098
00:49:54,520 --> 00:49:58,080
there's no perfect prescription or recipe for width versus depth

1099
00:49:58,080 --> 00:49:59,520
and what's optimal.

1100
00:49:59,520 --> 00:50:02,010
Yeah.

1101
00:50:02,010 --> 00:50:05,410
So the simplicity hypothesis in classical theory

1102
00:50:05,410 --> 00:50:08,570
basically says that big models learn complicated functions

1103
00:50:08,570 --> 00:50:09,990
and overfit the data.

1104
00:50:09,990 --> 00:50:13,450
And the emerging theory is that deep networks actually

1105
00:50:13,450 --> 00:50:16,027
learn simple functions that generalize.

1106
00:50:16,027 --> 00:50:18,610
And we're going to talk about this a lot more in a theoretical

1107
00:50:18,610 --> 00:50:21,510
and a more experimental lecture around generalization,

1108
00:50:21,510 --> 00:50:24,570
both in and out of distribution.

1109
00:50:24,570 --> 00:50:26,490
So again, what I've expected you to see

1110
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:29,970
before-- softmax, cross-entropy loss-- basically,

1111
00:50:29,970 --> 00:50:32,222
we have this model that we're trying

1112
00:50:32,222 --> 00:50:34,430
to use to learn to classify something like clownfish.

1113
00:50:34,430 --> 00:50:37,778
And we assume that we've seen some of these really basic loss

1114
00:50:37,778 --> 00:50:39,070
functions that we use for this.

1115
00:50:39,070 --> 00:50:43,210
So for example, if you have this as your last layer,

1116
00:50:43,210 --> 00:50:45,010
and now you have some set of categories

1117
00:50:45,010 --> 00:50:47,370
you're trying to learn to categorize over,

1118
00:50:47,370 --> 00:50:49,770
the simple version of this is you take the argmax,

1119
00:50:49,770 --> 00:50:52,490
and you say, OK, well, the thing that's darkest on here

1120
00:50:52,490 --> 00:50:54,450
is clownfish.

1121
00:50:54,450 --> 00:50:57,130
And then the loss that you would calculate

1122
00:50:57,130 --> 00:51:01,600
is, OK, the actual correct ground truth label is clownfish,

1123
00:51:01,600 --> 00:51:04,460
and then the error between those two, in this case, is zero.

1124
00:51:04,460 --> 00:51:05,400
So it's small.

1125
00:51:05,400 --> 00:51:08,860
So you've gotten the correct prediction.

1126
00:51:08,860 --> 00:51:12,300
And then maybe, actually, the real ground truth label

1127
00:51:12,300 --> 00:51:14,718
is grizzly bear, and you've said clownfish.

1128
00:51:14,718 --> 00:51:16,760
Now what you'd want is for your loss to be large.

1129
00:51:16,760 --> 00:51:18,580
So you want some loss function that

1130
00:51:18,580 --> 00:51:21,340
does a good job of punishing you when you're wrong

1131
00:51:21,340 --> 00:51:24,660
and not punishing you when you're right.

1132
00:51:24,660 --> 00:51:26,620
And the way we actually do this often

1133
00:51:26,620 --> 00:51:31,320
is if you assume the net output is some normalized vector,

1134
00:51:31,320 --> 00:51:33,380
then you can think of this as a probability

1135
00:51:33,380 --> 00:51:37,300
distribution over different possible object classes.

1136
00:51:37,300 --> 00:51:39,720
And so what you want this loss function on the right,

1137
00:51:39,720 --> 00:51:42,020
this is called cross-entropy loss.

1138
00:51:42,020 --> 00:51:43,980
This essentially indicates the distance

1139
00:51:43,980 --> 00:51:47,740
between what the model believes the output distribution should

1140
00:51:47,740 --> 00:51:50,760
be and what the input distribution actually is,

1141
00:51:50,760 --> 00:51:53,840
which in this case is what we call a one hot encoding,

1142
00:51:53,840 --> 00:51:56,860
or essentially a vector that's 0 everywhere except for the place

1143
00:51:56,860 --> 00:51:59,238
where it's correct, where it's 1.

1144
00:51:59,238 --> 00:52:01,280
And so essentially, it's just telling the network

1145
00:52:01,280 --> 00:52:04,400
to maximize the probability of the training data, which

1146
00:52:04,400 --> 00:52:07,200
forces the output to be a reasonably good probability

1147
00:52:07,200 --> 00:52:09,940
model of the object class, given the image,

1148
00:52:09,940 --> 00:52:12,280
assuming you have enough training data.

1149
00:52:12,280 --> 00:52:15,140
So what this looks like in practice, you say clownfish,

1150
00:52:15,140 --> 00:52:17,960
the ground truth label is clownfish.

1151
00:52:17,960 --> 00:52:22,600
Maybe this is your prediction, so now the score

1152
00:52:22,600 --> 00:52:24,720
is basically saying, this is how much better

1153
00:52:24,720 --> 00:52:27,040
you could have done--

1154
00:52:27,040 --> 00:52:28,740
how much better you could have done,

1155
00:52:28,740 --> 00:52:31,760
which is the difference between the amount of probability

1156
00:52:31,760 --> 00:52:33,000
you gave the class clownfish.

1157
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:34,420
I'm putting probability in quotes here.

1158
00:52:34,420 --> 00:52:35,880
It's super important to know that these are not

1159
00:52:35,880 --> 00:52:36,900
true probabilities.

1160
00:52:36,900 --> 00:52:39,360
They're scores.

1161
00:52:39,360 --> 00:52:42,360
And then that red is the dimension

1162
00:52:42,360 --> 00:52:43,473
that you could do better.

1163
00:52:43,473 --> 00:52:45,640
And that's telling you the direction of the gradient

1164
00:52:45,640 --> 00:52:47,960
you want to go in.

1165
00:52:47,960 --> 00:52:50,780
So maybe grizzly bear, you got it correct.

1166
00:52:50,780 --> 00:52:51,620
It's grizzly bear.

1167
00:52:51,620 --> 00:52:58,000
Now the amount that's left over is small versus actually

1168
00:52:58,000 --> 00:53:02,487
if it's chameleon, and you're saying iguana.

1169
00:53:02,487 --> 00:53:04,070
Essentially, this thing is normalized,

1170
00:53:04,070 --> 00:53:06,330
so if you put a lot more probability on one thing, then

1171
00:53:06,330 --> 00:53:07,788
the correct answer, then you end up

1172
00:53:07,788 --> 00:53:10,330
with a much smaller amount that now you're looking

1173
00:53:10,330 --> 00:53:12,810
at the difference between it.

1174
00:53:12,810 --> 00:53:16,170
And basically, the idea with all of this in deep learning

1175
00:53:16,170 --> 00:53:18,730
is you're taking all these different weights

1176
00:53:18,730 --> 00:53:23,170
in your massive model, and then you're fiddling around with them

1177
00:53:23,170 --> 00:53:25,170
until you match the desired output for all

1178
00:53:25,170 --> 00:53:26,410
your training data.

1179
00:53:26,410 --> 00:53:29,650
You're basically just wiggling around all

1180
00:53:29,650 --> 00:53:30,810
these different weights.

1181
00:53:30,810 --> 00:53:33,390
So you're going through your training data,

1182
00:53:33,390 --> 00:53:36,250
you're predicting something, you're calculating a loss,

1183
00:53:36,250 --> 00:53:38,995
and you're doing this for many different data samples.

1184
00:53:38,995 --> 00:53:40,370
And you're doing it over and over

1185
00:53:40,370 --> 00:53:42,330
and over again until the model gets everything

1186
00:53:42,330 --> 00:53:46,930
right, or as close to everything right as possible.

1187
00:53:46,930 --> 00:53:48,810
We also expect that you've seen some amount

1188
00:53:48,810 --> 00:53:52,370
of parallel processing and the idea of tensors before this.

1189
00:53:52,370 --> 00:53:54,570
So this is the idea that a lot of this stuff,

1190
00:53:54,570 --> 00:53:56,950
because it's repeated calculations,

1191
00:53:56,950 --> 00:53:58,310
you can be done in batch.

1192
00:53:58,310 --> 00:54:01,430
So each of these individual losses for the individual data

1193
00:54:01,430 --> 00:54:03,722
points, well, it's all going to be summed anyway.

1194
00:54:03,722 --> 00:54:05,430
So you can do it in parallel, and now you

1195
00:54:05,430 --> 00:54:09,950
can take these batches and more efficiently calculate that sum.

1196
00:54:09,950 --> 00:54:13,950
And so look, you take the same layer for all three of these,

1197
00:54:13,950 --> 00:54:16,310
you can stack them, and now essentially you

1198
00:54:16,310 --> 00:54:20,470
get this matrix that's features multiplied by images.

1199
00:54:20,470 --> 00:54:22,630
And so that's what your tensors are.

1200
00:54:22,630 --> 00:54:24,550
They're these multi-dimensional arrays,

1201
00:54:24,550 --> 00:54:26,910
and every single layer is some representation

1202
00:54:26,910 --> 00:54:29,230
of the input data.

1203
00:54:29,230 --> 00:54:31,090
And actually, everything is a tensor.

1204
00:54:31,090 --> 00:54:33,470
A tensor is just a multi-dimensional matrix.

1205
00:54:33,470 --> 00:54:36,990
So you can say, OK, here's our mathematical representation

1206
00:54:36,990 --> 00:54:38,190
of this model.

1207
00:54:38,190 --> 00:54:40,810
But you can also just represent it this way.

1208
00:54:40,810 --> 00:54:44,650
You have these vectors of your different input values,

1209
00:54:44,650 --> 00:54:46,330
and now you have your batch dimension,

1210
00:54:46,330 --> 00:54:49,610
which is now this vertical stacking of all those layers.

1211
00:54:49,610 --> 00:54:52,070
And now you multiply that by your weights matrix,

1212
00:54:52,070 --> 00:54:53,790
and you get out your output, and then

1213
00:54:53,790 --> 00:54:55,810
you run that through a pointwise non-linearity,

1214
00:54:55,810 --> 00:54:58,460
and then again, just another matrix multiplication.

1215
00:54:58,460 --> 00:55:00,480
And then you get your output z2, and then you

1216
00:55:00,480 --> 00:55:02,320
take that non-linearity, and you get y.

1217
00:55:02,320 --> 00:55:05,480
So this is why the fact that GPUs

1218
00:55:05,480 --> 00:55:07,480
can do so many multiplications in parallel

1219
00:55:07,480 --> 00:55:10,640
was important, because we can rewrite all of these models

1220
00:55:10,640 --> 00:55:13,140
as just essentially sets of multiplications.

1221
00:55:13,140 --> 00:55:15,040
And we'll go into this in a lot more detail

1222
00:55:15,040 --> 00:55:16,490
as well in the future.

1223
00:55:16,490 --> 00:55:18,240
So what we're going to cover in this class

1224
00:55:18,240 --> 00:55:20,740
is how deep networks represent data.

1225
00:55:20,740 --> 00:55:29,120
So essentially, it's this idea, where

1226
00:55:29,120 --> 00:55:33,280
deep networks are a more compact way of representing knowledge

1227
00:55:33,280 --> 00:55:34,400
in data.

1228
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:39,360
And previous methods we saw were more like lookup tables.

1229
00:55:39,360 --> 00:55:41,160
But there's other and maybe better ways

1230
00:55:41,160 --> 00:55:43,400
to learn a mapping between input and output

1231
00:55:43,400 --> 00:55:47,640
that basically assume that there are these lower-level building

1232
00:55:47,640 --> 00:55:50,880
blocks that are useful for many different possible downstream

1233
00:55:50,880 --> 00:55:51,680
tasks.

1234
00:55:51,680 --> 00:55:53,880
So one nice example is the idea of trying

1235
00:55:53,880 --> 00:55:56,290
to learn a classifier for the letter T.

1236
00:55:56,290 --> 00:55:58,470
So if you want to learn to classify the letter T,

1237
00:55:58,470 --> 00:56:00,762
you could try to build a classifier just for the letter

1238
00:56:00,762 --> 00:56:03,690
T. Or you could take a classifier that classifies

1239
00:56:03,690 --> 00:56:06,450
lines, and you could say, OK, here's a line,

1240
00:56:06,450 --> 00:56:09,090
and then you could rotate, have a rotation of that classifier,

1241
00:56:09,090 --> 00:56:10,530
and you'd say, here's a line.

1242
00:56:10,530 --> 00:56:13,410
And now all you have to do is understand the connection point

1243
00:56:13,410 --> 00:56:14,550
between these two lines.

1244
00:56:14,550 --> 00:56:19,770
So that line classifier is reusable in some combination

1245
00:56:19,770 --> 00:56:21,390
to get a T classifier.

1246
00:56:21,390 --> 00:56:22,830
And that's the basic idea here.

1247
00:56:22,830 --> 00:56:27,550
And actually in this post hoc way,

1248
00:56:27,550 --> 00:56:29,915
we've analyzed what is learned in layers

1249
00:56:29,915 --> 00:56:31,790
of things like convolutional neural networks.

1250
00:56:31,790 --> 00:56:34,690
And we do see that you'll get low-level representations

1251
00:56:34,690 --> 00:56:35,870
in the earlier layers.

1252
00:56:35,870 --> 00:56:38,330
Then they get combined into more and more complex

1253
00:56:38,330 --> 00:56:42,290
representations in those later layers.

1254
00:56:42,290 --> 00:56:44,150
And you can actually see this as well.

1255
00:56:44,150 --> 00:56:48,170
If you take an input image, and you take those,

1256
00:56:48,170 --> 00:56:52,650
and you cluster them in some low-level representation

1257
00:56:52,650 --> 00:56:56,002
of the space for each layer, the layers earlier on in the network

1258
00:56:56,002 --> 00:56:58,710
are not going to cluster well by category, because they're really

1259
00:56:58,710 --> 00:57:00,350
just categorizing things like, does it

1260
00:57:00,350 --> 00:57:04,550
have directional orientations of light and dark gradients

1261
00:57:04,550 --> 00:57:05,710
in the pixels?

1262
00:57:05,710 --> 00:57:08,210
But actually, when you get towards the end of the network,

1263
00:57:08,210 --> 00:57:09,910
it's learning more concepts, and that's

1264
00:57:09,910 --> 00:57:12,327
where you start getting these clusters of different things

1265
00:57:12,327 --> 00:57:14,550
like fish.

1266
00:57:14,550 --> 00:57:19,750
And we will talk about actually how that representation learns

1267
00:57:19,750 --> 00:57:23,390
and is structured through space in a bunch of different lectures

1268
00:57:23,390 --> 00:57:25,430
on representation learning, where we talk about

1269
00:57:25,430 --> 00:57:29,655
reconstruction, similarity, and theory.

1270
00:57:29,655 --> 00:57:32,030
We're also going to cover generative models going forward

1271
00:57:32,030 --> 00:57:33,350
in the class.

1272
00:57:33,350 --> 00:57:35,670
And this is things like text-based and image-based

1273
00:57:35,670 --> 00:57:37,070
generation.

1274
00:57:37,070 --> 00:57:39,470
And we'll cover basics, representations,

1275
00:57:39,470 --> 00:57:42,670
and conditional models.

1276
00:57:42,670 --> 00:57:44,590
And then we're going to cover in this class

1277
00:57:44,590 --> 00:57:46,870
how to think about weight reuse and some

1278
00:57:46,870 --> 00:57:48,670
of the efficiencies in terms of training

1279
00:57:48,670 --> 00:57:50,630
if you're able to reuse components

1280
00:57:50,630 --> 00:57:53,800
of previously trained models.

1281
00:57:53,800 --> 00:57:57,380
So here, again, this idea that, all right,

1282
00:57:57,380 --> 00:58:02,320
well, you learned this thing to categorize pictures of animals,

1283
00:58:02,320 --> 00:58:04,560
and now maybe you want to categorize

1284
00:58:04,560 --> 00:58:06,620
some sort of satellite imagery, well,

1285
00:58:06,620 --> 00:58:10,360
actually, a lot of those initial components about lines

1286
00:58:10,360 --> 00:58:13,000
and orientations and structures, those

1287
00:58:13,000 --> 00:58:14,620
are useful for both of these.

1288
00:58:14,620 --> 00:58:19,320
So what do we really need to learn from scratch versus what

1289
00:58:19,320 --> 00:58:22,160
is generally useful in terms of the representations that

1290
00:58:22,160 --> 00:58:24,343
are learned?

1291
00:58:24,343 --> 00:58:25,760
And this is really valuable if you

1292
00:58:25,760 --> 00:58:28,180
don't have big data or big compute,

1293
00:58:28,180 --> 00:58:31,240
if you can pre-generate representations that then you

1294
00:58:31,240 --> 00:58:34,320
can learn on top of without needing to learn everything

1295
00:58:34,320 --> 00:58:36,920
